WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:25.990 --> 00:00:32.800
There was a period in history between 1898
and 1922 dubbed the Heroic Age of Antarctic

00:00:32.800 --> 00:00:33.910
Exploration.

00:00:33.910 --> 00:00:38.000
It marked a time when daring adventurers set
their sights on one of the few lands that

00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:41.870
still escaped humanity’s reach - the Antarctic.

00:00:41.870 --> 00:00:47.760
And this land was harsh, cold, and unforgiving,
and all the people who dared face this unknown

00:00:47.760 --> 00:00:50.730
knew there was a good chance they might not
come back.

00:00:50.730 --> 00:00:55.680
Generally, 17 expeditions are considered part
of this era which made legends of men such

00:00:55.680 --> 00:00:59.850
as Ernest Shackleton, Douglas Mawson, and
Robert Falcon Scott.

00:00:59.850 --> 00:01:04.150
And, of course, there was Roald Amundsen,
the man we will be talking about today.

00:01:04.150 --> 00:01:08.250
As the leader of the very first expedition
to reach the South Pole, Amundsen secured

00:01:08.250 --> 00:01:10.619
his spot as one of the greatest explorers
in history.

00:01:10.619 --> 00:01:15.159
But that was just one of his polar adventures,
as Amundsen was the type of man destined to

00:01:15.159 --> 00:01:19.090
explore the frozen wilds forever.

00:01:19.090 --> 00:01:23.640
Early Years

00:01:23.640 --> 00:01:29.760
Roald Engelbregt Gravning Amundsen was born
on July 16, 1872, in the Norwegian parish

00:01:29.760 --> 00:01:34.520
of Borge, near the capital of Oslo which,
back then, was called Christiania.

00:01:34.520 --> 00:01:41.009
He was the youngest of four sons of Jens Engeberth
Amundsen and Hanna Gustava Sahlqvist, part

00:01:41.009 --> 00:01:46.120
of a well-off maritime family who ran a shipping
business which, at its height, operated 22

00:01:46.120 --> 00:01:47.120
vessels.

00:01:47.120 --> 00:01:51.049
Soon after Roald’s birth, the family relocated
to Oslo where the four boys grew up.

00:01:51.049 --> 00:01:53.479
Both parents wanted Roald to get a good education.

00:01:53.479 --> 00:01:58.119
His mother, in particular, wanted him to avoid
life on the sea altogether and become a doctor.

00:01:58.119 --> 00:02:03.009
But young Roald grew up with stories of famous
adventurers and explorers and wanted to emulate

00:02:03.009 --> 00:02:04.009
them.

00:02:04.009 --> 00:02:07.540
In school, he heard the tale of Franklin’s
lost expedition - how British explorer John

00:02:07.540 --> 00:02:11.840
Franklin set off to find a way to traverse
the Northwest Passage and was never heard

00:02:11.840 --> 00:02:12.840
from again.

00:02:12.840 --> 00:02:17.230
The Northwest Passage is a sea route that
connects the Atlantic and Pacific Oceans by

00:02:17.230 --> 00:02:19.250
passing through the Arctic Archipelago.

00:02:19.250 --> 00:02:24.760
For centuries, charting this passage was a
holy grail for European sailors as it represented

00:02:24.760 --> 00:02:29.620
a new, faster route to Asia, but many failed
in their attempts to safely navigate it.

00:02:29.620 --> 00:02:34.950
Franklin set off with two ships, the HMS Erebus
and HMS Terror, and vanished.

00:02:34.950 --> 00:02:39.510
Search attempts were all in vain and it wasn’t
until only a few years ago that both wrecks

00:02:39.510 --> 00:02:40.920
were finally located.

00:02:40.920 --> 00:02:44.560
Amundsen dreamt of succeeding where Franklin
failed.

00:02:44.560 --> 00:02:50.170
Then, in 1889, the 17-year-old Roald was in
the crowd to witness the glorious return of

00:02:50.170 --> 00:02:54.950
another great Norwegian explorer, Fridtjof
Nansen, after he became the first to traverse

00:02:54.950 --> 00:02:56.440
the interior of Greenland.

00:02:56.440 --> 00:03:01.820
Amundsen now knew that one day he wanted to
be a polar explorer just like his heroes.

00:03:01.820 --> 00:03:06.430
Speaking of which, you can probably expect
to see Fridtjof Nansen featured in his own

00:03:06.430 --> 00:03:09.231
Biographics video sometime in the future.

00:03:09.231 --> 00:03:12.650
But getting back to Amundsen, he was torn
between two decisions.

00:03:12.650 --> 00:03:15.690
On one hand, his life’s ambition was to
become an explorer.

00:03:15.690 --> 00:03:18.700
On the other, he promised his mother that
he would study medicine.

00:03:18.700 --> 00:03:22.910
In the end, he felt it was his duty to obey
his mother’s wishes so, in 1890, he enrolled

00:03:22.910 --> 00:03:26.970
in the Royal Norwegian Frederick University
of Christiania to become a doctor.

00:03:26.970 --> 00:03:32.340
However, when his mother died in 1893, Roald
ended his studies and set off on his true

00:03:32.340 --> 00:03:33.340
passion.

00:03:33.340 --> 00:03:34.610
It was probably for the best, though.

00:03:34.610 --> 00:03:38.590
Not only did Amundsen end up becoming one
of the world’s greatest explorers, but it

00:03:38.590 --> 00:03:42.680
was unlikely he would have ever graduated
from med school as he always had terrible

00:03:42.680 --> 00:03:45.240
grades and showed little aptitude for book-learning.

00:03:45.240 --> 00:03:49.910
The First Adventure

00:03:49.910 --> 00:03:56.210
That same year, Amundsen set off on his first
adventure - a ski tour over the Hardanger

00:03:56.210 --> 00:04:01.220
mountain plateau with two other men, Laurentius
Urdahl and Wilhelm Holst.

00:04:01.220 --> 00:04:05.900
The inexperience of the skiers became evident
and the trio had to abandon their journey.

00:04:05.900 --> 00:04:10.590
It wasn’t until five years later that Amundsen
attempted the ski tour again, this time successfully,

00:04:10.590 --> 00:04:12.790
accompanied by his older brother, Leon.

00:04:12.790 --> 00:04:17.209
Of course, in order to become a proper explorer,
life on land would not suffice.

00:04:17.209 --> 00:04:19.660
Roald also had to master sailing.

00:04:19.660 --> 00:04:25.820
Therefore, in 1894, he obtained his first
berth as an ordinary seaman aboard a seal-hunting

00:04:25.820 --> 00:04:27.860
ship called the Magdalena.

00:04:27.860 --> 00:04:34.830
This vessel was later renamed the Danmark
and was used by Danish explorer Ludvig Mylius-Erichsen

00:04:34.830 --> 00:04:39.760
in 1906 in his ill-fated expedition to charter
the unknown northeast coastline of Greenland,

00:04:39.760 --> 00:04:43.939
a mission which cost him his life and the
lives of his two senior officers.

00:04:43.939 --> 00:04:48.289
As a sailor, Amundsen performed his work admirably
and quickly rose through the ranks.

00:04:48.289 --> 00:04:52.940
Two years later, he got his first opportunity
at a true polar adventure when he was taken

00:04:52.940 --> 00:04:58.319
on as a mate aboard the RV Belgica for the
Belgian Antarctic Expedition.

00:04:58.319 --> 00:05:03.750
Captained by Belgian navy officer Adrien de
Gerlache, this voyage was a scientific mission

00:05:03.750 --> 00:05:04.849
to the Antarctic.

00:05:04.849 --> 00:05:09.110
Incidentally, it is also considered to be
the first expedition of the aforementioned

00:05:09.110 --> 00:05:11.590
Heroic Age of Antarctic Exploration.

00:05:11.590 --> 00:05:15.620
Roald Amundsen was not the only future explorer
aboard the vessel.

00:05:15.620 --> 00:05:18.789
The ship’s physician was an American named
Frederick Cook.

00:05:18.789 --> 00:05:22.979
He and Amundsen struck a lifelong friendship
and Cook would later achieve his own fame

00:05:22.979 --> 00:05:26.830
(or, should we say, notoriety) when he claimed
to be the first person to reach the North

00:05:26.830 --> 00:05:27.830
Pole.

00:05:27.830 --> 00:05:31.600
His claim was challenged by his main rival,
Robert Peary, and the question of who actually

00:05:31.600 --> 00:05:36.539
reached the geographic North Pole first is
still unsettled and highly controversial.

00:05:36.539 --> 00:05:38.879
But more on that later…

00:05:38.879 --> 00:05:43.229
As far as the Belgian expedition was concerned,
things were far from smooth sailing.

00:05:43.229 --> 00:05:47.219
The crew of the Belgica became the first people
to spend the winter in the Antarctic.

00:05:47.219 --> 00:05:51.090
This wasn’t by choice, mind you, but rather
because the ship became trapped in pack ice

00:05:51.090 --> 00:05:53.210
in the Bellingshausen Sea.

00:05:53.210 --> 00:05:58.720
The Belgica and its crew were stuck in these
icy conditions for 13 months, including two

00:05:58.720 --> 00:06:00.840
months of perpetual darkness.

00:06:00.840 --> 00:06:05.129
Some of the men, including de Gerlache, fell
gravely ill with scurvy while others began

00:06:05.129 --> 00:06:06.479
losing their sanity.

00:06:06.479 --> 00:06:11.360
It wasn’t until the start of 1899 that they
finally drifted near a stretch of open water.

00:06:11.360 --> 00:06:16.310
It was still hundreds of yards out of reach,
so the men had to saw and dynamite through

00:06:16.310 --> 00:06:21.870
their icy prison for a month in order to create
a channel for the ship to be able to move.

00:06:21.870 --> 00:06:26.469
The expedition finally reached the Belgian
capital of Antwerp on November 5, 1899, where

00:06:26.469 --> 00:06:30.729
the men received a hero’s welcome.

00:06:30.729 --> 00:06:35.129
The Northwest Passage

00:06:35.129 --> 00:06:39.400
Despite the many hardships, the Belgian Antarctic
Expedition was actually considered a success

00:06:39.400 --> 00:06:43.760
as the crew collected a lot of scientific
data and discovered many new islands.

00:06:43.760 --> 00:06:48.590
Moreover, it left Amundsen confident enough
that he was ready to lead his own expedition

00:06:48.590 --> 00:06:53.220
and he even obtained the endorsement of his
hero, Fridtjof Nansen, who often gave the

00:06:53.220 --> 00:06:57.249
young explorer advice and even, on occasion,
helped him secure financing.

00:06:57.249 --> 00:07:01.509
As far as his objective was concerned, Amundsen
was not one for half measures.

00:07:01.509 --> 00:07:05.339
Even though it was his first time as captain,
he wanted to fulfill his greatest ambition

00:07:05.339 --> 00:07:08.039
- navigating through the Northwest Passage.

00:07:08.039 --> 00:07:12.249
It was something that had been attempted many
times, but never succeeded all the way.

00:07:12.249 --> 00:07:16.870
Moreover, it was something very dangerous,
which had claimed the lives of explorers far

00:07:16.870 --> 00:07:18.479
more experienced than Amundsen.

00:07:18.479 --> 00:07:22.419
That being said, the Norwegian adventurer
didn’t simply plunge into this mission headfirst

00:07:22.419 --> 00:07:23.629
and hope for the best.

00:07:23.629 --> 00:07:24.629
He did his homework.

00:07:24.629 --> 00:07:30.089
In 1900, he visited German scientist and polar
explorer Georg von Neumayer who taught him

00:07:30.089 --> 00:07:34.219
what he knew about the planet’s magnetic
field and how it affected instruments at the

00:07:34.219 --> 00:07:35.219
poles.

00:07:35.219 --> 00:07:39.270
While the geographic North Pole is the northernmost
point on Earth, from which all directions

00:07:39.270 --> 00:07:43.479
point south, the magnetic North Pole moves
around all the time thanks to changes in the

00:07:43.479 --> 00:07:44.479
planet’s core.

00:07:44.479 --> 00:07:48.319
In fact, according to modern estimates, ever
since its formal discovery by British explorer

00:07:48.319 --> 00:07:53.990
James Clark Ross in 1831, the magnetic North
Pole has traveled around 1,400 miles.

00:07:53.990 --> 00:07:57.560
Amundsen didn’t need that kind of uncertainty
when heading in the right direction could

00:07:57.560 --> 00:08:00.439
make the difference between life and death.

00:08:00.439 --> 00:08:02.150
Next up, Amundsen needed a ship.

00:08:02.150 --> 00:08:05.919
He wanted something small and sturdy, but
it also had to be cheap because he did not

00:08:05.919 --> 00:08:07.750
have a lot of funds at his discretion.

00:08:07.750 --> 00:08:10.699
In the end, he opted for a 45-ton sloop called
Gjøa.

00:08:10.699 --> 00:08:14.719
It was much smaller than any ship that had
been used in the Northwest Passage before,

00:08:14.719 --> 00:08:19.460
but Amundsen felt that its flexibility and
increased maneuverability suited his purposes.

00:08:19.460 --> 00:08:23.339
Moreover, he also intended to use a very small
crew of only six people.

00:08:23.339 --> 00:08:29.030
By comparison, Franklin’s lost expedition
had two ships and 129 crewmen, but Amundsen

00:08:29.030 --> 00:08:33.100
concluded this was one of the missions’
fatal flaws as it severely stretched their

00:08:33.100 --> 00:08:34.210
limited resources.

00:08:34.210 --> 00:08:37.800
Moreover, it was his belief that every party
should only ever have the smallest number

00:08:37.800 --> 00:08:40.510
of people possible needed to achieve its goals.

00:08:40.510 --> 00:08:44.510
This way, everybody always had a clear assignment
and they felt that their efforts were vital

00:08:44.510 --> 00:08:46.150
to the success of the mission.

00:08:46.150 --> 00:08:51.010
In 1901, Amundsen had a ship, but still felt
unprepared to tackle the challenge he set

00:08:51.010 --> 00:08:52.010
in front of him.

00:08:52.010 --> 00:08:55.510
He spent the next two years making a trip
to the waters of Greenland to get acquainted

00:08:55.510 --> 00:09:00.089
with his new vessel, plus to test out equipment,
provisions, and the crewmen he intended to

00:09:00.089 --> 00:09:01.680
take on his expedition.

00:09:01.680 --> 00:09:06.120
This sapped Amundsen’s funds and, against
his wishes, he had to reach out to creditors.

00:09:06.120 --> 00:09:09.279
This was a problem that will haunt him for
most of his life.

00:09:09.279 --> 00:09:13.550
According to one version of the story, Amundsen
and his crew had to sneak out of Oslo when

00:09:13.550 --> 00:09:17.620
they started their mission as one of his creditors
intended to place the Gjøa under lien until

00:09:17.620 --> 00:09:18.990
they were repaid.

00:09:18.990 --> 00:09:22.800
Another version says that Fridtjof Nansen
had promised to take care of the debt while

00:09:22.800 --> 00:09:24.199
Amundsen was at sea.

00:09:24.199 --> 00:09:30.100
Whatever the truth might be, the Gjøa set
sail on June 16, 1903, headed for the Northwest

00:09:30.100 --> 00:09:31.100
Passage.

00:09:31.100 --> 00:09:33.860
The journey did not start off very auspiciously.

00:09:33.860 --> 00:09:37.250
In the early parts of the expedition, there
was a fire in the ship’s engine room and

00:09:37.250 --> 00:09:40.110
the vessel almost shipwrecked on an underwater
rock.

00:09:40.110 --> 00:09:42.350
After these initial problems were dealt with,
however, things went a lot smoother as the

00:09:42.350 --> 00:09:46.630
Gjøa crossed Baffin Bay and then managed
to navigate the multiple tight straits of

00:09:46.630 --> 00:09:48.370
the Arctic Archipelago.

00:09:48.370 --> 00:09:52.820
Some speculate that Amundsen could have made
the crossing in just one season, but the navigating

00:09:52.820 --> 00:09:56.550
of the Northwest Passage was just one of the
expedition’s goals.

00:09:56.550 --> 00:10:00.870
Other missions included collecting a ton of
scientific data and locating the new position

00:10:00.870 --> 00:10:02.089
of the magnetic North Pole.

00:10:02.089 --> 00:10:06.519
For this, the crew needed more time, so they
anchored in the natural harbor of King William

00:10:06.519 --> 00:10:09.399
Island and let the ship get iced in.

00:10:09.399 --> 00:10:14.680
Fortunately for them, the island had one human
settlement called Uqsuqtuuq which meant “lots

00:10:14.680 --> 00:10:19.250
of fat” in the Inuktitut language, thus
named due to the abundance of seals in the

00:10:19.250 --> 00:10:20.250
area.

00:10:20.250 --> 00:10:23.769
The local Inuit people were happy to host
the Norwegian crew and showed them many useful

00:10:23.769 --> 00:10:28.470
tips and techniques to survive the freezing
cold, tips which Amundsen took to heart and

00:10:28.470 --> 00:10:31.750
would later employ during his conquest of
the South Pole.

00:10:31.750 --> 00:10:36.160
The explorers spent almost two years there
before concluding their objectives and also

00:10:36.160 --> 00:10:39.440
finding weather conditions suitable enough
to allow them to leave.

00:10:39.440 --> 00:10:43.620
Internationally, the settlement is now better
known as Gjoa Haven for the role it played

00:10:43.620 --> 00:10:45.540
in making polar history.

00:10:45.540 --> 00:10:48.139
There were still more obstacles to overcome.

00:10:48.139 --> 00:10:50.530
Near the Yukon, the ship got iced in again.

00:10:50.530 --> 00:10:54.890
Worst of all, while Amundsen took a sled ride
to Eagle, Alaska, to send some telegrams,

00:10:54.890 --> 00:10:59.839
one of his men, Gustav Wiik, fell ill and
died on March 31, 1906.

00:10:59.839 --> 00:11:04.269
Amundsen later described him as the happiest
one in the group, always “full of humor

00:11:04.269 --> 00:11:08.649
and stories”, who proved invaluable to maintaining
morale on the voyage.

00:11:08.649 --> 00:11:13.029
On the stranger side of things, Amundsen also
found out on this occasion that the nation

00:11:13.029 --> 00:11:16.769
and king that he served when he left on the
trip weren’t the same as the ones waiting

00:11:16.769 --> 00:11:17.790
for him when he returned.

00:11:17.790 --> 00:11:22.620
We’ve referred to Roald Amundsen as being
from Norway, although, technically, up until

00:11:22.620 --> 00:11:27.699
this point, he was actually a citizen of the
United Kingdoms of Sweden and Norway.

00:11:27.699 --> 00:11:33.199
Ever since 1814, the two countries had been
in a personal union where they kept separate

00:11:33.199 --> 00:11:38.060
constitutions and administrations, but were
ruled by the same king.

00:11:38.060 --> 00:11:40.430
In Amundsen’s time, that was King Oscar
II of Sweden.

00:11:40.430 --> 00:11:45.610
However, the nations underwent a peaceful
dissolution in 1905 so, when Amundsen returned

00:11:45.610 --> 00:11:50.610
home, he returned to a fully independent Norway
ruled by King Haakon VII.

00:11:50.610 --> 00:11:55.970
Obstacles, tragedies, and political movements
aside, the Gjøa reached Nome, Alaska on August

00:11:55.970 --> 00:12:27.660
31, 1906, officially completing its navigation
of the Northwest Passage.

00:12:27.660 --> 00:13:09.370
Headed for

00:13:09.370 --> 00:13:20.649
the North Pole

00:13:20.649 --> 00:13:25.410
Daring feats like crossing the Northwest Passage
might make you famous and celebrated, but

00:13:25.410 --> 00:13:26.410
they won’t make you rich.

00:13:26.410 --> 00:13:30.180
For the next couple of years, Amundsen engaged
in writing and giving lectures to improve

00:13:30.180 --> 00:13:33.579
his financial situation, but he disliked these
activities intensely.

00:13:33.579 --> 00:13:38.709
What he wanted was to leave on another adventure,
not to sit behind a desk, but he had debts

00:13:38.709 --> 00:13:39.709
to pay.

00:13:39.709 --> 00:13:42.910
Even given his newfound fame, Amundsen was
having trouble finding creditors willing to

00:13:42.910 --> 00:13:43.940
finance him.

00:13:43.940 --> 00:13:47.310
What he needed was a new plan that will stir
up public interest.

00:13:47.310 --> 00:13:52.670
In 1908, he approached the Norwegian Geographic
Society with a proposition - he wanted to

00:13:52.670 --> 00:13:58.050
take the Fram (the ship that Fridtjof Nansen
used in his voyages) and recreate the latter’s

00:13:58.050 --> 00:14:02.620
expedition to the Arctic Ocean, this time
with better preparation and more modern equipment.

00:14:02.620 --> 00:14:07.829
This plan was received with enthusiasm by
the society, by the public, and by the king

00:14:07.829 --> 00:14:11.440
who became one of the first donors to Amundsen’s
fundraising campaign.

00:14:11.440 --> 00:14:15.000
This was at a time when there were multiple
expeditions undertaken to reach the North

00:14:15.000 --> 00:14:16.120
and South Poles.

00:14:16.120 --> 00:14:21.589
American explorer Robert Peary set a new Farthest
North record in 1906 while British adventurer

00:14:21.589 --> 00:14:27.370
Ernest Shackleton was just 97 nautical miles
from reaching the South Pole in 1909.

00:14:27.370 --> 00:14:31.399
Although it wasn’t explicitly stated during
the fundraising, it was widely believed that

00:14:31.399 --> 00:14:35.769
Roald Amundsen would also make an attempt
for the North Pole, perhaps becoming the first

00:14:35.769 --> 00:14:39.259
man to succeed in reaching one of the most
remote points on the planet.

00:14:39.259 --> 00:14:44.120
The plan went down the drain in late 1909
when a new story reached the headlines - Frederick

00:14:44.120 --> 00:14:48.290
Cook, Amundsen’s former shipmate aboard
the Belgica, had reached the North Pole.

00:14:48.290 --> 00:14:52.100
His claim was attacked by Robert Peary who
also claimed to have reached the North Pole,

00:14:52.100 --> 00:14:53.939
although he did it slightly later.

00:14:53.939 --> 00:14:57.910
This launched a whole mess of arguments and
controversies that hasn’t been completely

00:14:57.910 --> 00:14:59.000
sorted even today.

00:14:59.000 --> 00:15:05.170
Cook’s claim was dismissed by many not necessarily
as false, but as being unproven, while Peary’s

00:15:05.170 --> 00:15:10.279
was accepted, but with a lot of hesitation,
and many experts have retroactively dismissed

00:15:10.279 --> 00:15:11.279
his claim, as well.

00:15:11.279 --> 00:15:12.959
None of this mattered in the moment, though.

00:15:12.959 --> 00:15:16.699
Either one, or both, or neither of them reached
the North Pole, but the headlines said they

00:15:16.699 --> 00:15:21.970
did so, all of a sudden, it wasn’t seen
as the “holy grail” of polar exploration

00:15:21.970 --> 00:15:22.970
anymore.

00:15:22.970 --> 00:15:27.110
It had been done which meant that interest
in Amundsen’s planned expedition started

00:15:27.110 --> 00:15:29.759
to dwindle and the fundraising dried up.

00:15:29.759 --> 00:15:33.899
This was around the same time that Shackleton
tried and failed to reach the South Pole so

00:15:33.899 --> 00:15:38.560
Amundsen thought that maybe he could still
salvage his expedition by changing his destination

00:15:38.560 --> 00:15:39.970
from the North to the South Pole.

00:15:39.970 --> 00:15:41.640
He had strong competition.

00:15:41.640 --> 00:15:45.959
Another British explorer, Robert Falcon Scott,
or Scott of the Antarctic, as he later came

00:15:45.959 --> 00:15:50.270
to be known, had already made preparations
and announced his intentions to sail for the

00:15:50.270 --> 00:15:53.290
South Pole should Shackleton not succeed.

00:15:53.290 --> 00:15:57.850
His party, the Terra Nova Expedition, left
Cardiff in June, 1910.

00:15:57.850 --> 00:16:01.540
On his end, Amundsen had decided to make the
switch, but did not tell anyone apart from

00:16:01.540 --> 00:16:02.990
a few close confidants.

00:16:02.990 --> 00:16:07.209
He was heavily in debt and had to mortgage
his own house to fund the expedition after

00:16:07.209 --> 00:16:09.260
newspapers cancelled their sponsorships.

00:16:09.260 --> 00:16:13.430
He knew that only a sensational feat will
help him raise funds and that wasn’t to

00:16:13.430 --> 00:16:15.410
be found at the North Pole anymore.

00:16:15.410 --> 00:16:19.339
If he went public with his new intentions,
he risked having his expedition delayed or

00:16:19.339 --> 00:16:23.579
cancelled altogether, thus giving Scott the
best chance at succeeding.

00:16:23.579 --> 00:16:27.889
Amundsen knew he would be criticized for his
underhanded tactic and, indeed, he was.

00:16:27.889 --> 00:16:32.050
Mainly, he was accused of entering a race
against Scott which was not only unscientific,

00:16:32.050 --> 00:16:36.470
but also endangered the men of both expeditions.

00:16:36.470 --> 00:16:40.620
A Change of Plans

00:16:40.620 --> 00:16:46.399
The Fram left Norway on August 9, eight weeks
after the Terra Nova Expedition.

00:16:46.399 --> 00:16:50.019
At this point, most of the 19 crewmen aboard
the ship still thought they were headed for

00:16:50.019 --> 00:16:51.019
the North Pole.

00:16:51.019 --> 00:16:55.269
It wasn’t until they stopped at Madeira
for new provisions that Amundsen informed

00:16:55.269 --> 00:16:59.399
them of his new plan and also wrote a telegram
to tell Scott, as well.

00:16:59.399 --> 00:17:03.990
The South Pole Expedition reached Antarctica
in January, 1911, and set up a base camp on

00:17:03.990 --> 00:17:08.300
a natural ice harbor called the Bay of Whales
on the Ross Ice Shelf.

00:17:08.300 --> 00:17:10.720
They named this camp Framheim Station.

00:17:10.720 --> 00:17:15.589
At one point, they even met a small party
from the Terra Nova Expedition who was exploring

00:17:15.589 --> 00:17:16.589
the area.

00:17:16.589 --> 00:17:20.250
On the surface, relations between the two
groups were cordial, although Amundsen later

00:17:20.250 --> 00:17:24.510
confessed that he was relieved when he discovered
that the British crew had no wireless radio

00:17:24.510 --> 00:17:28.790
as he feared they might have been able to
send word of their success first even if he

00:17:28.790 --> 00:17:30.730
reached the South Pole before them.

00:17:30.730 --> 00:17:35.529
Here was where Amundsen’s previous experiences
in polar situations, as well as the techniques

00:17:35.529 --> 00:17:38.350
he learned from the Inuit people, came into
play.

00:17:38.350 --> 00:17:42.380
He and his men wore furred skins like the
Inuits instead of the traditional heavy wool

00:17:42.380 --> 00:17:43.380
clothing.

00:17:43.380 --> 00:17:47.290
They subsided on a meat-rich diet as Amundsen
observed during his stint aboard the Belgica

00:17:47.290 --> 00:17:49.039
that it helped ward off scurvy.

00:17:49.039 --> 00:17:54.419
Most importantly, the Norwegian relied completely
on dog sleds and skis for transportation.

00:17:54.419 --> 00:17:58.809
He brought almost 100 Greenland dogs with
him on his trip as they could also be used

00:17:58.809 --> 00:18:00.140
for meat along the way.

00:18:00.140 --> 00:18:04.350
Scott, on the other hand, had a certain aversion
to using dogs as he considered them unreliable.

00:18:04.350 --> 00:18:09.170
He employed a mixed transportation method
consisting of dogs, Siberian ponies, and motorized

00:18:09.170 --> 00:18:10.170
sledges.

00:18:10.170 --> 00:18:15.250
The sledges were supposed to be the ace up
his sleeve, except they were the exact opposite.

00:18:15.250 --> 00:18:17.920
First of all, one of them they lost while
unloading the ship.

00:18:17.920 --> 00:18:22.070
Furthermore, they increased the cargo load
since the party had to carry fuel for them.

00:18:22.070 --> 00:18:26.330
But most importantly, they weren’t capable
of handling the extreme weather and the others

00:18:26.330 --> 00:18:28.340
broke down early into the trip.

00:18:28.340 --> 00:18:32.309
By comparison, Amundsen’s journey went a
lot smoother, although he, too, flirted with

00:18:32.309 --> 00:18:33.690
disaster once.

00:18:33.690 --> 00:18:37.500
After months of setting up the Framheim Station,
a party of eight set off for the South Pole

00:18:37.500 --> 00:18:39.540
in September, 1911.

00:18:39.540 --> 00:18:42.050
This was done only to get a headstart on the
British.

00:18:42.050 --> 00:18:45.700
Amundsen soon realized that they set off too
early and should have waited for the polar

00:18:45.700 --> 00:18:47.830
spring for better weather conditions.

00:18:47.830 --> 00:18:51.400
They returned to Framheim, but lost a lot
of good dogs on the way and several of the

00:18:51.400 --> 00:18:53.150
men were sidelined with severe frostbite.

00:18:53.150 --> 00:18:58.220
It also led to an argument between Amundsen
and one of his officers, Hjalmar Johansen,

00:18:58.220 --> 00:19:01.570
who accused him of acting foolishly to beat
Scott.

00:19:01.570 --> 00:19:06.159
Unsurprisingly, Johansen was left out of the
next South Pole party that departed a month

00:19:06.159 --> 00:19:07.159
later.

00:19:07.159 --> 00:19:11.740
This revised party only included five men,
as well as 52 dogs and four sledges.

00:19:11.740 --> 00:19:16.700
The men were Roald Amundsen, Helmer Hanssen
who had also been part of his Northwest Passage

00:19:16.700 --> 00:19:20.659
crew, Olav Bjaaland, Oscar Wisting, and Sverre
Hassel.

00:19:20.659 --> 00:19:24.130
These were the first men to reach the South
Pole, thanks partly to finding a good, clear

00:19:24.130 --> 00:19:28.920
route on top of a previously-undiscovered
glacier which Amundsen named Axel Heiberg

00:19:28.920 --> 00:19:29.920
Glacier.

00:19:29.920 --> 00:19:33.470
On December 14, 1911, the party arrived at
their destination.

00:19:33.470 --> 00:19:38.190
They raised a little tent they named Polheim,
planted the Norwegian flag, and left behind

00:19:38.190 --> 00:19:42.420
a letter for Scott or whomever else may find
it should they fail to return.

00:19:42.420 --> 00:19:46.480
They were very diligent in their calculations,
wanting to make sure they had the right spot

00:19:46.480 --> 00:19:50.610
and avoid a situation like that between Peary
and Cook up in the North Pole.

00:19:50.610 --> 00:19:54.400
Scott’s party arrived a month later on January
18, 1912.

00:19:54.400 --> 00:19:58.659
Amundsen’s team made it back to Framheim
in late January and quickly began packing

00:19:58.659 --> 00:20:03.180
everything up on the ship to leave for Australia
and send word of their success.

00:20:03.180 --> 00:20:06.950
Of course, Amundsen didn’t know it at the
time, but there was no reason to hurry as

00:20:06.950 --> 00:20:11.580
Scott’s entire party tragically died on
the way back.

00:20:11.580 --> 00:20:17.059
North Pole by Air

00:20:17.059 --> 00:20:21.650
When he was back home, Amundsen once again
began planning a drift expedition on the Arctic

00:20:21.650 --> 00:20:24.679
Ocean like Fridtjof Nansen had done before
him.

00:20:24.679 --> 00:20:29.020
He bought and fitted a new ship named Maud,
but his plans were put on hold by World War

00:20:29.020 --> 00:20:30.020
I.

00:20:30.020 --> 00:20:33.309
He eventually set off in 1918, but this turned
out to be his worst venture ever.

00:20:33.309 --> 00:20:36.140
Problems kept cropping up that caused delay
after delay.

00:20:36.140 --> 00:20:39.710
Six years had passed and the expedition had
not fulfilled its mission.

00:20:39.710 --> 00:20:44.070
Amundsen was, once again, heavily in debt
and all his creditors had lost their patience

00:20:44.070 --> 00:20:47.559
with him and demanded repayment, including
his own brother.

00:20:47.559 --> 00:20:53.200
The explorer had no other choice - in September,
1924, he declared bankruptcy and lost his

00:20:53.200 --> 00:20:54.200
ship.

00:20:54.200 --> 00:20:57.820
For a while, it seemed like Amundsen’s adventuring
days had come to an end, as he began giving

00:20:57.820 --> 00:21:00.260
lectures in the United States to make money.

00:21:00.260 --> 00:21:05.090
However, the opportunity for one last hurrah
presented itself in the form of Lincoln Ellsworth,

00:21:05.090 --> 00:21:08.780
a rich American with an interest in polar
exploration.

00:21:08.780 --> 00:21:13.380
Ellsworth was willing to fund more expeditions
to the North Pole, as long as he was part

00:21:13.380 --> 00:21:14.380
of the group.

00:21:14.380 --> 00:21:18.409
In the previous years, Amundsen had tried
and failed to fly over the Pole but now, with

00:21:18.409 --> 00:21:21.330
Ellsworth’s backing, he was ready to try
again.

00:21:21.330 --> 00:21:27.010
First, in 1925, Amundsen, Ellsworth, and four
other men took two Dornier Do J flying boats

00:21:27.010 --> 00:21:32.640
and almost reached 88° north which, at the
time, was the northernmost latitude ever achieved

00:21:32.640 --> 00:21:33.640
by plane.

00:21:33.640 --> 00:21:37.370
They had to stop short because one of the
aircrafts was damaged and had to land.

00:21:37.370 --> 00:21:41.299
However, just a year later, Amundsen and a
crew of 16 went aboard an Italian airship

00:21:41.299 --> 00:21:45.380
called the Norge and successfully flew over
the geographic North Pole.

00:21:45.380 --> 00:21:50.150
Assuming that both Peary and Cook’s controversial
claims of reaching the North Pole were incorrect,

00:21:50.150 --> 00:21:54.880
as well as that of an American pilot named
Richard Byrd who also made a disputed claim

00:21:54.880 --> 00:21:58.610
of flying over the Pole, this would be the
first verified successful attempt of reaching

00:21:58.610 --> 00:22:00.520
the geographic North Pole.

00:22:00.520 --> 00:22:04.740
Even if it wasn’t, the flight still made
Amundsen (and one of his trusted companions,

00:22:04.740 --> 00:22:09.390
Oscar Wisting), the first men to travel to
both the North and South Poles.

00:22:09.390 --> 00:22:13.900
In the end, Roald Amundsen considered that
that was about as good as it got and he finally

00:22:13.900 --> 00:22:15.370
retired, although not for long.

00:22:15.370 --> 00:22:20.570
Just two years later, the 55-year-old Amundsen
and a team of five boarded a Latham 47 flying

00:22:20.570 --> 00:22:23.580
boat to assist in the search &amp; rescue of a
crashed airship.

00:22:23.580 --> 00:22:26.880
Amundsen’s aircraft disappeared on June
18, 1928.

00:22:26.880 --> 00:22:31.640
It is presumed that it crashed in the Barents
Sea, although the bodies of Roald Amundsen

00:22:31.640 --> 00:22:51.769
and his crew were never found.

